{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017byV3kCAAS2obSGAyYXsMj","type":"tool_result","content":"\"\"\"Story library: the publish handler.\n\nEach story is stored in one DynamoDB item together with the three most similar\nstories already in the library (``similar``), so the reader page can show\n\"related\" without a query at read time.\n\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport re\nimport time\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"stories\")\n\nTABLE_NAME = \"stories\"\nTOP_N = 3\n\n_WORD = re.compile(r\"[a-z0-9]+\")\n\n\ndef words(text):\n    return set(_WORD.findall((text or \"\").lower()))\n\n\ndef similarity(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Shared-tag ratio (Jaccard), blended with shared words of the two bodies.\"\"\"\n    ta, tb = set(a.get(\"tags\", [])), set(b.get(\"tags\", []))\n    tag_score = len(ta & tb) / len(ta | tb) if (ta | tb) else 0\n    wa, wb = words(a.get(\"body\")), words(b.get(\"body\"))\n    word_score = len(wa & wb) / len(wa | wb) if (wa | wb) else 0\n    return round(0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score, 4)\n\n\ndef related(story, existing):\n    scored = []\n    for other in existing:\n        if other[\"slug\"] == story[\"slug\"]:\n            continue\n        scored.append({\"slug\": other[\"slug\"], \"score\": similarity(story, other)})\n    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"score\"], reverse=True)\n    return scored[:TOP_N]\n\n\ndef validate(story):\n    for k in (\"slug\", \"title\", \"body\"):\n        if not story.get(k):\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing field: {k}\")\n    if not re.fullmatch(r\"[a-z0-9-]{3,80}\", story[\"slug\"]):\n        raise ValueError(\"bad slug\")\n\n\ndef publish(table, story):\n    \"\"\"Store ``story`` with its related stories. Returns the stored item.\"\"\"\n    validate(story)\n    existing = table.scan(TableName=TABLE_NAME).get(\"Items\", [])\n    item = {\n        \"slug\": story[\"slug\"],\n        \"title\": story[\"title\"],\n        \"tags\": sorted(set(story.get(\"tags\", []))),\n        \"body\": story[\"body\"],\n        \"published_at\": int(time.time()),\n        \"similar\": related(story, existing),\n    }\n    table.put_item(Item=item)\n    log.info(\"publish slug=%s similar=%d stored\", item[\"slug\"], len(item[\"similar\"]))\n    return item\n\n\ndef handler(event, table):\n    \"\"\"Lambda-style entry point for POST /stories. Body is the story as JSON.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        story = json.loads(event.get(\"body\") or \"{}\")\n        stored = publish(table, story)\n        return {\"statusCode\": 201, \"body\": json.dumps({\"slug\": stored[\"slug\"]})}\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return {\"statusCode\": 400, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": str(e)})}\n    except Exception:\n        log.exception(\"publish failed\")\n        return {\"statusCode\": 500, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": \"Internal Server Error\"})}\n---TESTS\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport unittest\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), \"..\", \"src\"))\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(__file__))\n\nimport app  # noqa: E402\nfrom fake_dynamo import FakeTable  # noqa: E402\n\n\nclass PublishTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_publish_stores_story(self):\n        table = FakeTable()\n        story = {\n            \"slug\": \"why-my-cron-ran-twice\",\n            \"title\": \"Why my cron ran twice\",\n            \"tags\": [\"cron\", \"linux\"],\n            \"body\": \"Two crontabs, one for root and one for me, both had the line.\",\n        }\n        stored = app.publish(table, story)\n        self.assertEqual(stored[\"slug\"], \"why-my-cron-ran-twice\")\n        self.assertEqual(table.get_item(Key={\"slug\": \"why-my-cron-ran-twice\"})[\"Item\"][\"title\"], \"Why my cron ran twice\")\n        self.assertEqual(table.scan()[\"Count\"], 1)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n---FAKE\n\"\"\"In-memory stand-in for a boto3 DynamoDB Table resource.\n\nIt stores items in a dict and validates values the way boto3's TypeSerializer\ndoes, so a test fails on the same inputs a real table would reject.\n\"\"\"\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\n\ndef _check(value, path=\"Item\"):\n    if isinstance(value, bool) or value is None or isinstance(value, (int, str, bytes, Decimal)):\n        return\n    if isinstance(value, float):\n        raise TypeError(\"Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\")\n    if isinstance(value, dict):\n        for k, v in value.items():\n            _check(v, f\"{path}.{k}\")\n        return\n    if isinstance(value, (list, tuple, set, frozenset)):\n        for i, v in enumerate(value):\n            _check(v, f\"{path}[{i}]\")\n        return\n    raise TypeError(f\"Unsupported type {type(value)!r} for value at {path}\")\n\n\nclass FakeTable:\n    def __init__(self, key=\"slug\"):\n        self.key = key\n        self.items = {}\n\n    def put_item(self, Item, **kwargs):\n        _check(Item)\n        if self.key not in Item:\n            raise ValueError(f\"item is missing the key attribute {self.key!r}\")\n        self.items[Item[self.key]] = dict(Item)\n        return {\"ResponseMetadata\": {\"HTTPStatusCode\": 200}}\n\n    def get_item(self, Key, **kwargs):\n        item = self.items.get(Key[self.key])\n        return {\"Item\": dict(item)} if item is not None else {}\n\n    def scan(self, **kwargs):\n        return {\"Items\": [dict(v) for v in self.items.values()], \"Count\": len(self.items)}\n---NOTES\n---\ntitle: The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\ndate: 2026-07-31\ntags: dynamodb, python, testing, aws-lambda, boto3\ntype: success\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\n\nMy publish endpoint worked perfectly — once. The first story went in clean. The\nsecond one returned Internal Server Error, and the log said:\n\"TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\"\n\nDynamoDB does not accept Python floats. Everyone learns this eventually; the\ninteresting part is why the first write survived. My API computes similarity\nscores between the incoming story and the existing ones, and stores the top\nmatches. On the very first publish the table was empty — no matches, no scores,\nno floats, no crash. The bug did not exist until the data did.\n\nThe fix took one line (scores became integer percentages — DynamoDB is happy with\nints and Decimals). The lesson took longer to sink in: my \"it works\" test had only\never exercised the empty-database path. Any code whose behavior depends on\nexisting state — scoring, deduplication, aggregation, \"related items\" — has a\nsecond code path that first-write tests never touch.\n\nNow every write-path test seeds the table with one record before testing the\nwrite. The populated path is the real path; the empty path happens exactly once\nin production, ever.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: any Python service writing to DynamoDB via boto3.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the location of your put_item / update_item calls,\nand your test setup.\n\nSteps: trace every value that feeds a DynamoDB write and flag anything computed —\ndivisions, averages, rounded scores — because in Python those are floats; convert\nto int or decimal.Decimal at the boundary; then add one integration test that\nseeds a record and writes a second one, so state-dependent code (similarity,\ndedup, counters) actually executes under test.\n\nVerify: grep your codebase for round( and / feeding item dicts; run the\nseeded-table test; publish twice against a real table — the second write is the\none that tells the truth.\n---README\n# stories-api\n\nA small story library behind `POST /stories` (Lambda + DynamoDB table `stories`,\npartition key `slug`). `src/app.py` is the whole service: `handler()` parses\nthe request and `publish()` stores the story together with its three most\nsimilar existing stories (`similar: [{slug, score}]`), so the reader page can\nshow \"related\" without any query at read time. `logs/error.log` is the\nproduction log from the first two publishes after the deploy.\n\nTests use an in-memory table (`tests/fake_dynamo.py`) that validates items the\nway boto3 does, so no AWS account is needed. Run them with\n`python3 -m unittest discover tests` from the repo root.\n---GI\n__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n---\ntotal 8\ndrwx------   9 valentinakoniukhova wheel  288 Oct 20 20:39 .\ndrwxrwxrwt 242 root                wheel 7744 Oct 20 20:39 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  12 valentinakoniukhova wheel  384 Oct 20 20:39 .git\n-rw-r--r--   1 valentinakoniukhova wheel   19 Oct 20 20:39 .gitignore\n-rw-r--r--   1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  654 Oct 20 20:39 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x   3 valentinakoniukhova wheel   96 Oct 20 20:39 logs\ndrwxr-xr-x   5 valentinakoniukhova wheel  160 Oct 20 20:39 notes\ndrwxr-xr-x   3 valentinakoniukhova wheel   96 Oct 20 20:39 src\ndrwxr-xr-x   4 valentinakoniukhova wheel  128 Oct 20 20:39 tests","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d64c7773-2dc6-403c-a576-86ef103b550f","uuid":"c125cac9-4e40-41ce-8f5e-750ade7a2246","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:39:22.195Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\"\"\"Story library: the publish handler.\n\nEach story is stored in one DynamoDB item together with the three most similar\nstories already in the library (``similar``), so the reader page can show\n\"related\" without a query at read time.\n\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport re\nimport time\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"stories\")\n\nTABLE_NAME = \"stories\"\nTOP_N = 3\n\n_WORD = re.compile(r\"[a-z0-9]+\")\n\n\ndef words(text):\n    return set(_WORD.findall((text or \"\").lower()))\n\n\ndef similarity(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Shared-tag ratio (Jaccard), blended with shared words of the two bodies.\"\"\"\n    ta, tb = set(a.get(\"tags\", [])), set(b.get(\"tags\", []))\n    tag_score = len(ta & tb) / len(ta | tb) if (ta | tb) else 0\n    wa, wb = words(a.get(\"body\")), words(b.get(\"body\"))\n    word_score = len(wa & wb) / len(wa | wb) if (wa | wb) else 0\n    return round(0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score, 4)\n\n\ndef related(story, existing):\n    scored = []\n    for other in existing:\n        if other[\"slug\"] == story[\"slug\"]:\n            continue\n        scored.append({\"slug\": other[\"slug\"], \"score\": similarity(story, other)})\n    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"score\"], reverse=True)\n    return scored[:TOP_N]\n\n\ndef validate(story):\n    for k in (\"slug\", \"title\", \"body\"):\n        if not story.get(k):\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing field: {k}\")\n    if not re.fullmatch(r\"[a-z0-9-]{3,80}\", story[\"slug\"]):\n        raise ValueError(\"bad slug\")\n\n\ndef publish(table, story):\n    \"\"\"Store ``story`` with its related stories. Returns the stored item.\"\"\"\n    validate(story)\n    existing = table.scan(TableName=TABLE_NAME).get(\"Items\", [])\n    item = {\n        \"slug\": story[\"slug\"],\n        \"title\": story[\"title\"],\n        \"tags\": sorted(set(story.get(\"tags\", []))),\n        \"body\": story[\"body\"],\n        \"published_at\": int(time.time()),\n        \"similar\": related(story, existing),\n    }\n    table.put_item(Item=item)\n    log.info(\"publish slug=%s similar=%d stored\", item[\"slug\"], len(item[\"similar\"]))\n    return item\n\n\ndef handler(event, table):\n    \"\"\"Lambda-style entry point for POST /stories. Body is the story as JSON.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        story = json.loads(event.get(\"body\") or \"{}\")\n        stored = publish(table, story)\n        return {\"statusCode\": 201, \"body\": json.dumps({\"slug\": stored[\"slug\"]})}\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return {\"statusCode\": 400, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": str(e)})}\n    except Exception:\n        log.exception(\"publish failed\")\n        return {\"statusCode\": 500, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": \"Internal Server Error\"})}\n---TESTS\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport unittest\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), \"..\", \"src\"))\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(__file__))\n\nimport app  # noqa: E402\nfrom fake_dynamo import FakeTable  # noqa: E402\n\n\nclass PublishTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_publish_stores_story(self):\n        table = FakeTable()\n        story = {\n            \"slug\": \"why-my-cron-ran-twice\",\n            \"title\": \"Why my cron ran twice\",\n            \"tags\": [\"cron\", \"linux\"],\n            \"body\": \"Two crontabs, one for root and one for me, both had the line.\",\n        }\n        stored = app.publish(table, story)\n        self.assertEqual(stored[\"slug\"], \"why-my-cron-ran-twice\")\n        self.assertEqual(table.get_item(Key={\"slug\": \"why-my-cron-ran-twice\"})[\"Item\"][\"title\"], \"Why my cron ran twice\")\n        self.assertEqual(table.scan()[\"Count\"], 1)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n---FAKE\n\"\"\"In-memory stand-in for a boto3 DynamoDB Table resource.\n\nIt stores items in a dict and validates values the way boto3's TypeSerializer\ndoes, so a test fails on the same inputs a real table would reject.\n\"\"\"\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\n\ndef _check(value, path=\"Item\"):\n    if isinstance(value, bool) or value is None or isinstance(value, (int, str, bytes, Decimal)):\n        return\n    if isinstance(value, float):\n        raise TypeError(\"Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\")\n    if isinstance(value, dict):\n        for k, v in value.items():\n            _check(v, f\"{path}.{k}\")\n        return\n    if isinstance(value, (list, tuple, set, frozenset)):\n        for i, v in enumerate(value):\n            _check(v, f\"{path}[{i}]\")\n        return\n    raise TypeError(f\"Unsupported type {type(value)!r} for value at {path}\")\n\n\nclass FakeTable:\n    def __init__(self, key=\"slug\"):\n        self.key = key\n        self.items = {}\n\n    def put_item(self, Item, **kwargs):\n        _check(Item)\n        if self.key not in Item:\n            raise ValueError(f\"item is missing the key attribute {self.key!r}\")\n        self.items[Item[self.key]] = dict(Item)\n        return {\"ResponseMetadata\": {\"HTTPStatusCode\": 200}}\n\n    def get_item(self, Key, **kwargs):\n        item = self.items.get(Key[self.key])\n        return {\"Item\": dict(item)} if item is not None else {}\n\n    def scan(self, **kwargs):\n        return {\"Items\": [dict(v) for v in self.items.values()], \"Count\": len(self.items)}\n---NOTES\n---\ntitle: The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\ndate: 2026-07-31\ntags: dynamodb, python, testing, aws-lambda, boto3\ntype: success\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\n\nMy publish endpoint worked perfectly — once. The first story went in clean. The\nsecond one returned Internal Server Error, and the log said:\n\"TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\"\n\nDynamoDB does not accept Python floats. Everyone learns this eventually; the\ninteresting part is why the first write survived. My API computes similarity\nscores between the incoming story and the existing ones, and stores the top\nmatches. On the very first publish the table was empty — no matches, no scores,\nno floats, no crash. The bug did not exist until the data did.\n\nThe fix took one line (scores became integer percentages — DynamoDB is happy with\nints and Decimals). The lesson took longer to sink in: my \"it works\" test had only\never exercised the empty-database path. Any code whose behavior depends on\nexisting state — scoring, deduplication, aggregation, \"related items\" — has a\nsecond code path that first-write tests never touch.\n\nNow every write-path test seeds the table with one record before testing the\nwrite. The populated path is the real path; the empty path happens exactly once\nin production, ever.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: any Python service writing to DynamoDB via boto3.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the location of your put_item / update_item calls,\nand your test setup.\n\nSteps: trace every value that feeds a DynamoDB write and flag anything computed —\ndivisions, averages, rounded scores — because in Python those are floats; convert\nto int or decimal.Decimal at the boundary; then add one integration test that\nseeds a record and writes a second one, so state-dependent code (similarity,\ndedup, counters) actually executes under test.\n\nVerify: grep your codebase for round( and / feeding item dicts; run the\nseeded-table test; publish twice against a real table — the second write is the\none that tells the truth.\n---README\n# stories-api\n\nA small story library behind `POST /stories` (Lambda + DynamoDB table `stories`,\npartition key `slug`). `src/app.py` is the whole service: `handler()` parses\nthe request and `publish()` stores the story together with its three most\nsimilar existing stories (`similar: [{slug, score}]`), so the reader page can\nshow \"related\" without any query at read time. `logs/error.log` is the\nproduction log from the first two publishes after the deploy.\n\nTests use an in-memory table (`tests/fake_dynamo.py`) that validates items the\nway boto3 does, so no AWS account is needed. Run them with\n`python3 -m unittest discover tests` from the repo root.\n---GI\n__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n---\ntotal 8\ndrwx------   9 valentinakoniukhova wheel  288 Oct 20 20:39 .\ndrwxrwxrwt 242 root                wheel 7744 Oct 20 20:39 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  12 valentinakoniukhova wheel  384 Oct 20 20:39 .git\n-rw-r--r--   1 valentinakoniukhova wheel   19 Oct 20 20:39 .gitignore\n-rw-r--r--   1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  654 Oct 20 20:39 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x   3 valentinakoniukhova wheel   96 Oct 20 20:39 logs\ndrwxr-xr-x   5 valentinakoniukhova wheel  160 Oct 20 20:39 notes\ndrwxr-xr-x   3 valentinakoniukhova wheel   96 Oct 20 20:39 src\ndrwxr-xr-x   4 valentinakoniukhova wheel  128 Oct 20 20:39 tests","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputEx